*(
    title: Complex Numbers
    description: Adjoining a square root of $-1$ to the reals, and the plane that turns the resulting arithmetic into geometry.
    tags: complex numbers, argand plane, modulus, argument, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Squaring a real number never produces a negative one. A positive number squares to a positive number, a negative number squares to a positive number, and zero squares to zero — so the parabola on the canvas sits on or above the horizontal axis at every point of its domain.

    ===

    That is why $x^2 = -1$ has no real solution: the rule at $y = -1$ runs under the curve and never touches it. The equation is not hard to solve, it is unsolvable in the numbers we have.

    The response is to stop looking and start building. Declare a new object whose square is $-1$, then insist that it obey the ordinary rules of algebra alongside the reals.

    definition{
        A number, written $i$, defined by the property $i^2 = -1$. It is not a real number, and it is not an approximation to one.
    }(
        term: Imaginary unit
    )

    Once $i$ is available, every expression $a + ib$ with $a$ and $b$ real is a number in its own right. The pair $(a, b)$ is the whole of it: two complex numbers are equal exactly when their real parts agree and their imaginary parts agree.

    definition{
        A number of the form $z = a + ib$, where $a$ and $b$ are real and $i^2 = -1$. The real number $a$ is its real part, written $\operatorname{Re}(z)$; the real number $b$ is its imaginary part, written $\operatorname{Im}(z)$.
    }(
        term: Complex number
    )

    note{
        $\operatorname{Im}(z)$ is the real number $b$, not $ib$. The imaginary part of $3 + 2i$ is $2$.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        curve(expression: x^2; colour: blue)
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: orange; label: smallest value of $x^2$)

        cue{
            axis.label(y: -1; label: $y = -1$; line)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $y$
        x domain: [-3, 3]
        y domain: [-2, 9]
    )
]

scene{
    # Arithmetic, with one new rule

    Addition needs no new idea. Real parts add to real parts, imaginary parts to imaginary parts, and subtraction works the same way.

    ===

    Multiplication is ordinary expansion until the last term. Every product of two imaginary parts throws up an $i^2$, and that is where the definition earns its keep: $-10i^2$ is not left standing, it becomes $+10$ and moves across to join the real part.

    ===

    Division is the case with no obvious answer, because $\frac{1}{1 - 5i}$ is not in the form $a + ib$ and cannot be read off. The tool is the conjugate — the same number with the sign of its imaginary part reversed.

    definition{
        For $z = a + ib$, the complex number $\bar{z} = a - ib$. Multiplying a complex number by its conjugate gives $(a + ib)(a - ib) = a^2 + b^2$, which is real and non-negative.
    }(
        term: Complex conjugate
    )

    ===

    So multiply top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator. The denominator collapses to the real number $26$, the numerator is an ordinary multiplication, and dividing through by $26$ leaves the quotient in $a + ib$ form. This is the same manoeuvre as rationalising a surd denominator, with $i$ in place of $\sqrt{2}$.
}[
    #### Addition: the two parts add separately

    equation{
        (3 + 2i) + (1 - 5i) = (3 + 1) + (2 - 5)i = 4 - 3i
    }

    cue{
        #### Multiplication: expand, then apply $i^2 = -1$

        equation{
            (3 + 2i)(1 - 5i) = 3 - 15i + 2i - 10i^2
        }

        equation{
            = (3 + 10) + (-15 + 2)i = 13 - 13i
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### A number times its conjugate is real

        equation{
            (1 - 5i)(1 + 5i) = 1 - 25i^2 = 1 + 25 = 26
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )

    cue{
        #### Division: multiply above and below by that conjugate

        equation{
            \frac{3 + 2i}{1 - 5i} = \frac{(3 + 2i)(1 + 5i)}{(1 - 5i)(1 + 5i)} = \frac{-7 + 17i}{26} = -\frac{7}{26} + \frac{17}{26}\,i
        }
    }(
        in: 3
    )
]

scene{
    # The Argand plane

    A complex number carries two independent real numbers, so one axis cannot hold it and two can. Plot $\operatorname{Re}(z)$ horizontally and $\operatorname{Im}(z)$ vertically and every complex number has a point, every point a complex number.

    definition{
        The Cartesian plane used to represent complex numbers, with the real part plotted on the horizontal axis — the real axis — and the imaginary part on the vertical axis, the imaginary axis.
    }(
        term: Argand plane
    )

    The two rules on the canvas meet at $z = 3 + 2i$. Read the horizontal one and you have $\operatorname{Re}(z)$; read the vertical one and you have $\operatorname{Im}(z)$. The real numbers are still present as the points sitting on the horizontal axis, where $\operatorname{Im}(z) = 0$.

    ===

    Conjugation now has a picture. Reversing the sign of the imaginary part leaves the horizontal coordinate alone and flips the vertical one, so $\bar{z}$ is the reflection of $z$ in the real axis. A number equal to its own conjugate is a number fixed by that reflection, which is to say a real one.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        point(x: 3; y: 2; colour: orange; label: $z = 3 + 2i$)
        axis.label(x: 3; label: $\operatorname{Re}(z)$; line)
        axis.label(y: 2; label: $\operatorname{Im}(z)$; line)

        cue{
            point(x: 3; y: -2; colour: purple; label: $\bar{z} = 3 - 2i$)
            axis.label(y: -2; label: $\operatorname{Im}(\bar{z})$; line)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
    )
]

scene{
    ## Modulus and argument

    Once $z$ is a point, the two numbers that locate it need not be its coordinates. Its distance from the origin and its direction from the origin fix it just as well, and those two turn out to govern multiplication far better than $a$ and $b$ do.

    definition{
        The distance from the origin to $z$ in the Argand plane. For $z = a + ib$ it is the non-negative real number $|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$.
    }(
        term: Modulus
    )

    The dashed legs on the canvas are $\operatorname{Re}(z)$ and $\operatorname{Im}(z)$, so the modulus is a hypotenuse: $|3 + 2i| = \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{13}$. This is also why $z\bar{z} = a^2 + b^2$ is worth naming — that product is exactly $|z|^2$, here $(3 + 2i)(3 - 2i) = 13$.

    The direction is the angle the teal segment makes with the positive real axis. For $3 + 2i$ that angle is $\arctan\frac{2}{3} \approx 0.588$ radians, or about $33.7^\circ$.

    definition{
        An angle $\theta$ measured anticlockwise from the positive real axis to the segment joining the origin to $z$, written $\arg z$. Adding any multiple of $2\pi$ gives the same direction, so $\arg z$ names a whole family of angles.
    }(
        term: Argument
    )

    To make the argument single-valued, one member of that family is picked out: the principal argument $\operatorname{Arg} z$, the one satisfying $-\pi < \operatorname{Arg} z \le \pi$. The modulus of $0$ is $0$ and its argument is undefined, since no segment leaves the origin.

    note{
        Reading $\arg z$ off $\arctan\frac{b}{a}$ alone loses the quadrant: $1 + i$ and $-1 - i$ share the ratio $\frac{b}{a} = 1$ but point in opposite directions. Sketch the point before trusting the calculator.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        segment(start: (0, 0); end: (3, 0); colour: grey; dashed)
        segment(start: (3, 0); end: (3, 2); colour: grey; dashed)
        segment(start: (0, 0); end: (3, 2); colour: teal; label: $|z| = \sqrt{13}$)
        point(x: 3; y: 2; colour: orange; label: $z$)
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
    )
]
